A regular hexagon has side length $s$ (equal to its circumradius). Place its center at the origin with vertices at angles $k\cdot 60^\circ$, $k=0,1,\dots,5$, i.e. at
$$V_k = s(\cos k60^\circ,\; \sin k60^\circ).$$

**Hexagon area.** A regular hexagon of side $s$ consists of 6 equilateral triangles of side $s$, so
$$A_{\text{hex}} = 6\cdot \tfrac{\sqrt3}{4}s^2 = \tfrac{3\sqrt3}{2}s^2 = 24 \;\Longrightarrow\; s^2 = \frac{16}{\sqrt3}.$$

**The rectangle.** We seek a rectangle whose four sides each contain at least one hexagon vertex, with all six vertices on the boundary. Take the rectangle with sides parallel to the coordinate axes (the "flat-top/bottom" orientation). Its sides are the extreme $x$- and $y$-values of the vertices:

- $x$-coordinates: $\cos(k60^\circ)\in\{1,\tfrac12,-\tfrac12,-1,-\tfrac12,\tfrac12\}$, so $\max x = s$ (vertex $V_0$), $\min x = -s$ (vertex $V_3$).
- $y$-coordinates: $\sin(k60^\circ)\in\{0,\tfrac{\sqrt3}{2},\tfrac{\sqrt3}{2},0,-\tfrac{\sqrt3}{2},-\tfrac{\sqrt3}{2}\}$, so $\max y = \tfrac{\sqrt3}{2}s$ (vertices $V_1,V_2$), $\min y = -\tfrac{\sqrt3}{2}s$ (vertices $V_4,V_5$).

**Every vertex lies on the boundary:**
| Vertex | $x$ | $y$ | on side |
|---|---|---|---|
| $V_0$ | $s=\max x$ | $0$ | right |
| $V_1$ | $\tfrac{s}{2}$ | $\tfrac{\sqrt3}{2}s=\max y$ | top |
| $V_2$ | $-\tfrac{s}{2}$ | $\tfrac{\sqrt3}{2}s=\max y$ | top |
| $V_3$ | $-s=\min x$ | $0$ | left |
| $V_4$ | $-\tfrac{s}{2}$ | $-\tfrac{\sqrt3}{2}s=\min y$ | bottom |
| $V_5$ | $\tfrac{s}{2}$ | $-\tfrac{\sqrt3}{2}s=\min y$ | bottom |

All six vertices touch the rectangle's perimeter, with each of the four sides carrying at least one vertex. The rectangle dimensions are
$$\text{width} = 2s, \qquad \text{height} = \sqrt3\, s.$$

**Rectangle area:**
$$A_{\text{rect}} = 2s \cdot \sqrt3\, s = 2\sqrt3\, s^2 = 2\sqrt3 \cdot \frac{16}{\sqrt3} = 32.$$

**Uniqueness (sketch).** Any valid rectangle has sides in directions $\alpha, \alpha+90^\circ$. A vertex $V_k$ lies on the boundary only if its projection onto one axis attains the global max or min of that projection over all six vertices. For a generic $\alpha$ (e.g. $\alpha=15^\circ$), the vertex at $60^\circ$ has projection $\cos 45^\circ\cdot s\approx 0.707s$ on the $\alpha$-axis and $\sin 45^\circ\cdot s\approx 0.707s$ on the $\alpha+90^\circ$ axis — neither equals the extreme value $\cos 15^\circ\cdot s\approx 0.966s$ — so it is strictly interior and the configuration fails. Exploiting the hexagon's $60^\circ$ rotational symmetry, the only admissible orientations in $[0^\circ,60^\circ)$ are $\alpha=0^\circ$ and $\alpha=30^\circ$; these are related by a $90^\circ$ rotation of the rectangle and both yield width $\times$ height $= 2\sqrt3\, s^2 = 32$. Hence the area is uniquely determined.

$$\boxed{A_{\text{rect}} = 32 \text{ square kilometers}}$$

